    "narrative_title": "Hormuz disruption drives a global oil supply shock and market volatility",
    "narrative_summary": "A stalemate over reopening the Strait of Hormuz and ongoing Middle East disruptions have become the dominant driver of rising oil prices, tighter physical supply (including lower exports and refinery strains), and market volatility. The situation is producing knock-on effects — strategic reserve draws, regional producer revenue swings, asset sales and diplomatic maneuvering — while accelerating short-term demand for alternatives and raising fiscal and inflationary pressures worldwide. Feedbacks between prices, producer output decisions, and geopolitical bargaining are keeping the system unstable and prolonging the shock.",
    "scope": "Crude production in the Middle East and elsewhere; maritime transit through the Strait of Hormuz; export flows, tanker and shipping risks; refining capacity and margins; inventories and strategic petroleum reserves; global oil pricing and derivatives markets; major state and corporate actors (Iran, US, Gulf producers, OPEC, national oil companies, supermajors); immediate policy responses and sanctions. Excludes unrelated commodity markets and purely domestic non-energy fiscal policy.",
    "super_system": "Global energy-economy and geopolitical/security system (international trade, finance, and strategic stability)",
    "adjacent_subsystems": [
      "International shipping, tanker insurance and chokepoint security",
      "Global financial and derivatives markets",
      "Energy transition and renewable deployment",
      "National fiscal and political systems (budgets, sanctions, domestic energy policy)"
    ],
    "evidence_headlines": [
      "Oil Surges With No Hormuz Deal in Sight",
      "Oil Rises Amid Growing Doubts That Strait of Hormuz Can Reopen Soon",
      "ADNOC Gas second-quarter profit falls 52% as Hormuz closure hits sales",
      "Oil in U.S. Strategic Petroleum Reserve falls below 300 million barrels, lowest since 1983",
      "Middle East oil output to remain below pre-conflict levels in 2027",
      "Refining crunch keeps fuel prices high as crude retreats"
    ]
